fix lock-based atomics: gensio_typeof() applied '->val' twice

The lock-based gensio_atomic_* fallback (used when neither C11
atomics nor GCC __atomic builtins are available, e.g. GCC 4.6 /
old MIPS targets with --std=gnu99) expands gensio_typeof((a)->val),
but gensio_typeof() already appends ->val to its argument, yielding
__typeof__(((a)->val)->val), i.e. '->' applied to an unsigned int:

  error: invalid type argument of '->' (have 'unsigned int')

reported from every gensio_refcount_* function that uses
gensio_atomic_inc/dec_if_nz (gensio_refcount.h:41 et al).

All call sites pass the value expression (a)->val, and the
non-typeof fallback (gensio_typeof(x) -> long) also expects a value
expression, so the typeof branches must be typeof(a)/__typeof__(a).
Call sites and all four branches are consistent afterwards; the
stdatomic and __atomic branches do not use gensio_typeof at all and
are unaffected.  Upstream bug, present in 2.8.15 and 3.0.4.

--- a/include/gensio/gensio_atomics.h
+++ b/include/gensio/gensio_atomics.h
@@ -36,15 +36,15 @@

 #if defined(__cplusplus) && __cplusplus >= 202302L
 /* c++ doesn't define __STDC_VERSION__ */
-#define gensio_typeof(a) typeof((a)->val)
+#define gensio_typeof(a) typeof(a)

 #elif defined(__STDC_VERSION__) && __STDC_VERSION__ >= 202311L
 /* For C only. */
-#define gensio_typeof(a) typeof((a)->val)
+#define gensio_typeof(a) typeof(a)

 #elif defined(__GNUC__) || defined(_MSC_VER)
 /* Use the built-in GCC one that always works with gcc and MSC. */
-#define gensio_typeof(a) __typeof__((a)->val)
+#define gensio_typeof(a) __typeof__(a)

 #else
 /* Just use the largest possible value. */
